.TH MD2DOCX-UC 1 "August 6, 2026" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
md2docx-uiuc \- Convert Markdown to Word document with University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign theme
.SH SYNOPSIS
md2docx-uiuc INPUT_FILE
.SH DESCRIPTION
md2docx-uiuc is an easy-to-use wrapper script that converts a Markdown file to a Microsoft Word .docx document using the UIUC-themed converter. It applies the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign branding (navy headings, orange hyperlinks) and automatically generates a table of contents.

This is a simplified interface to the underlying md2docx.py script with commonly used options pre-configured for UIUC documentation workflows.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fBINPUT_FILE\fP
Path to the Markdown file to convert. Must have .md extension.
.SH BEHAVIOR
The script converts INPUT_FILE to a Word document with the same basename in the same directory (e.g., notes.md → notes.docx). It uses the following md2docx.py options:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB-o INPUT_FILE.docx\fP: Specify output file path
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--toc\fP: Include table of contents in the generated document
.RE
If an output file already exists, it will be overwritten without warning.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Convert a single Markdown file:
    md2docx-uiuc meeting_notes.md
    # Creates meeting_notes.docx in the same directory
.PP
Convert a file in a subdirectory:
    md2docx-uiuc docs/project_overview.md
    # Creates docs/project_overview.docx
.SH ENVIRONMENT
No specific environment variables are required. The script assumes:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Python 3 is available in PATH
.IP \(bu 2
pandoc is installed (brew install pandoc)
.IP \(bu 2
python-docx is available (only needed for reference doc rebuilding)
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/md2docx.py\fP
The underlying Markdown to Word converter (uses pandoc + python-docx).
.TP
\fI~/md2docx-uiuc\fP
This wrapper script.
.TP
\fI~/.md2docx/reference.docx\fP
Cached UIUC reference document (auto-generated on first run).
.TP
\fIINPUT_FILE.docx\fP
The generated Word document output file (same directory as input).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0   Success.
1   Error (e.g., missing input file, missing md2docx.py, missing dependencies, or conversion failure).
